WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Good morning, John. It is our last video of the 20-teens.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:10.560
And I'm tempted to look back: at fidget spinners, at "The Dress", at planking - which was this decade, apparently?!

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.200
But I can't, because I'm too worried about the future and I feel like a lot of people are, so I want to talk about it.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.140
And of course, the future is worrying.
It is unknown and unknowable.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.840
But I also think that worry can get a bad rap,
because we do it wrong.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.660
So, let's talk about it.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.820
But first, in many ways, the world got better
over the last 10 years.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.100
Even if you just look at America,
the world got better over the last 10 years.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:39.029
I had a Motorola Droid in 2009. Eugh!
But also, bigger deal, gay marriage was illegal.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.740
Also, the unemployment rate went from 10 percent down to 4, and yet,

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.560
to me, now feels a bit shakier than then did.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.160
I'm not really sure why that is.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.660
Part of it has to be that we're starting to feel the real effects of climate change in two different ways.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.740
One: the actual real effect - the climate is changing.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.940
But then secondly, we have a growing populace
of young people,

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.000
knowing that they are gonna be the ones
who actually have to solve this problem,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.780
looking to their elected leaders, who aren't recognizing that the problem exists.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.260
So yeah, that's gonna cause some tension.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.980
Also, this is an election year, and it's gonna be stressful because of that. They're always stressful.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.120
And, also, we just impeached our president
about the fact that

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.180
he doesn't have a great deal of respect for the process.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.229
So yeah, I'm worried about that.

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:27.480
I'm also worried that this tension, some of which is actively manufactured by our leaders,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.940
or just the architecture of the social internet,
or by Russian bot farms,

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.860
will continue to pull at the bonds of community.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.460
And that's a story of the last decade, but beyond that
as well, which has had real negative impact.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:44.320
So I just threw a bunch of stuff at you.
I probably made you feel super stressed out.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.980
I apologize for that, but here's why.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.020
There's somewhat of a tendency to say, like,
2019 was such a trash year.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.840
There's no way 2020 could be worse, but like, look.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.920
I don't mean to continue to freak you out,
but it could always be worse.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.940
This is one of the reasons
why I like science fiction.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:04.720
It's a way to worry about the future,
to like play out scenarios in narrative form.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.260
Our first Life's Library book of this year,
Parable of the Sower by Octavia Butler,

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:10.760
great example of this.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:15.960
So I actually do worry that we might worry the wrong ways, but I also worry that we might not worry enough!

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.220
So have fun with that!

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.740
But here's what's up.
We're getting to the point.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:26.080
One of the smartest teachers I ever had once told me that what he looks for in good leaders is worry.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.420
People who are brave and bold, yes, but also
people who are kept up at night by the possibilities,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.040
who think and ruminate and fret.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.080
Because worry isn't a disease;
it's a symptom of caring.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.940
And it's a tool, it's a survival strategy.
Being able to think through a situation and assign

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:48.800
probabilities to different outcomes, however flawed those probabilities may be, is a human superpower!

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.780
It is the reason we are alive right now.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.260
I don't think all worry is healthy or anything.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:58.000
We do have to find the right ways to be worried
and the right things to worry about.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.040
We need worries that motivate us
and that are also based in reality,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.260
rather than ideology or just blind opposition.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.840
And also worry almost always works best
hand in hand with hope.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.440
So, yeah, I'm worried about 2020
because 2020 needs me to be worried about it,

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.230
just like I need my mom
to be worried about me.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:20.540
And I know that it can feel like we are powerless
in the face of so many other people

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.100
who want completely different or
slightly different things than we do,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.060
and who oftentimes have more
resources than we do.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.920
But we're not powerless. Who we donate to,
who we vote for, the conversations we have,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:40.420
how we choose to imagine the world: all of those
things are how the entire human earth gets built.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.280
So yes, I'm worried for the future,
and yes, I'm glad!

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.000
John, I'll see you in 2020.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.580
P.S. If you are in St. Petersburg, Atlanta,
or Raleigh, North Carolina,

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.340
and you want to come have a good time
and also maybe think some big thoughts with us,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.660
we're going on a little tour.
More information in the description.

